 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are a fixed contract; the certificates node uses only this interface and never reads registry storage.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Each kiosk keeps the active snapshot in a local embedded SQLite index keyed by the normalized serial number. `findBySerial` resolves a lookup with a single indexed query against that database. Snapshot validation, index preparation, and lookup all belong to this node; the index is private to the registry.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot and its SQLite index without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot and its index active and reports a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Query the active index with the normalized serial number and return `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`, including the active snapshot date in either result.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index, rebuilt on the kiosk when a signed snapshot is activated | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | All lookups must complete from files on the kiosk with no runtime network access, and kiosks have no network route during normal operation (HLD tenets), so both network-dependent options are inadmissible. SQLite gives predictable lookups with no service to run; its cost is that each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index when a snapshot arrives, and that work stays inside this node. |
